{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786481878,"nonce":"dLPevQspqMrCg/PUj1IxVkoY3fI=","title":"Title: `The fūrin existed as two hand-copied SVGs whose stroke widths differed with nothing recording why, and login carried no mark at all`","message":"**What is wrong.** oto's only piece of brand art existed twice, inline: a 20-unit `viewBox` at `size-4` in the header and a 40-unit `viewBox` at `size-8` in `EmptyState`. Their `stroke-width` values disagreed — 1.5 and 1.6 — and no comment said whether that was a copy drifting or a decision.\n\n**The demonstration.** It was a decision. 1.5 × (16/20) = 1.20 px; 1.6 × (32/40) = 1.28 px. Both copies were tuned to land on the same rendered hairline, so the obvious de-duplication — one `viewBox`, one stroke — silently halves the header (1.6 × 0.4 = 0.64 px). Separately, `routes/login.tsx` had no brand mark on the one screen with no incident on it.\n\n**Why it matters.** Undocumented optical compensation reads as inconsistency, and the fix for an inconsistency is to collapse it.\n\n**Where.** `web/src/components/ui/Chime.tsx` now owns the glyph with per-size stroke constants at `:61-64` and the arithmetic at `:9-28`; call sites are `AppShell.tsx:302`, `states.tsx:39` (16 `EmptyState` uses) and `login.tsx:82`.\n\n**Scope.** small\n\n**Done when.** Done. `Chime.test.tsx:52-86` pins 3 and 1.6 and asserts they differ — that pin is the deliverable, because a single uncommented stroke value is exactly how this returns.","files":null}]}